  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 305 AM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nEarly this morning, a weak cold front continues to push south \nacross the Northland, We will see lingering low clouds and fog \nwhich dissipate after sunrise. Today, gusty northeast winds are \nexpected to develop, as the cold front sags to our south and \nhigh pressure moving in from the north tightens the pressure \ngradient. Wind gusts of up to 35 mph are possible for the Twin \nPorts and South Shore areas during the daytime, with wind gusts \nof 20 to 25 mph farther inland. These winds will lead to another\nday of High Risk for rip currents for the Twin Ports beaches. A\nBeach Hazards Statement will again be needed for Sunday and \nalso likely again Monday. This surge will also bring cooler and \ndrier air into the region, with high temperatures near Lake \nSuperior only in the upper 50s, with 60s elsewhere. \n\nWe have been seeing signals for isolated lake effect showers in\nthe CAMs for today, but I suspect we do not yet have enough \ntemperature difference between the surface and 850mb for true \nlake effects. Later today, the drier air moving in from the \nnortheast will shut off this potential completely. \n\nClouds will clear from northwest to southeast tonight into \nMonday. Together with the high pressure building in with lighter\nwinds and the cooler airmass, means a small area of frost is \npossible for the Minnesota Borderlands and interior Arrowhead by\nearly Monday morning. Frost headlines may be needed for these \nlimited areas pending the exact sky cover trends today and this \nevening. Monday will feature mostly sunny skies but cool \nconditions with a repeat of 50s near Lake Superior and low-mid \n60s elsewhere. The northeast winds will likely give us another \nday with elevated rip current risk along the beaches of the Twin\nPorts. \n\nThe coldest night this week will be Monday night into Tuesday \nmorning, with mostly clear skies, and light winds with the \nsurface high pressure ridge overhead. This will lead to \nexcellent radiational cooling overnight, which should produce \nwidespread frost over much of the region, with even some sub-\nfreezing temperatures in our local cold spots. Frost and Freeze \nheadlines will likely be needed. Tuesday will remain sunny and \ncool with with high temperatures moderating slightly but still \non the cool side. Another round of frost is possible Tuesday \nnight into Wednesday morning with continued dry, calm and clear \nconditions. \n\nA gradual warming trends begins on Wednesday as the surface \nhigh pressure ridge slides east of the area, allowing warmer air\nand more moisture to move back into the area. A shortwave may \nbring some precipitation chances back into the area on Thursday,\nbut otherwise the forecast is generally dry for the rest of the\nwork week, with high temperatures rising into the 60s and low \n70s by Friday and Saturday. Next weekend there are some \nindications we may have some precipitation chances, but \nconfidence remains low with an upper level ridge in place and \ngenerally weak features.",
